A||||4||GT Bastard||Engine experts needed for 3.4 question||||5||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||01-11-2000||11:58 PM||||I'm beginning to consider taking apart the 'ol Fiero, but I don't think I want to keep it a 2.8, and I don't think the 3.1 is enough of an improvement.  My question is this:  What CAN and CAN'T be used from the 2.8 on the 3.4?  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phaeton||01-12-2000||12:40 AM||||A stock 3.1 is certainly no match for a stock 3.4, but...it is less expensive to stroke the 2.8 already owned and spend some dollars on machine work.  Balanced engine, pistons matched to bores, intakes and exhausts cleaned up under the valves.  headersbyed  I don't remember his whole address but headersbyed is in the middle, he is a mustread for anyone doing an engine swap needing new exhaust system, or just looking for more power.  A streetable 200hp is attainable by stroking and building 2.8 and for less money than buying and installing a 3.4 of equivalent power.||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||01-12-2000||01:16 AM||||The Fiero 2.8 and the Camaro/Firebird 3.4 are essentially the same motor.  The heads are identical, so you can use everything from the 3.4 except for the intake.  You will need a neutrally balanced flywheel (like the '88s used) and you will need to drill new starter holes, since this motor came from a rear wheel drive car, the starter is on the wrong side and will interfere with the axles.<P>Once the starter holes are drilled and the starter is relocated you can install the factory Fiero intake system..... you need to use the 3.4 fuel injectors and an adjustible fuel pressure regulator is a good idea too.  I also believe that the 3.4 has DIS, but the distributor hole is there...just remove the plug and install the Fiero distributor.  You might need to use a Fiero oil pan, but I'm not positive.<P>There are probably a few other minor things needed to finish it up (like the engine mounts/brackets), but that's most of it. <P>It really isn't a hard thing to do....the new starter holes are the biggest hurdle to overcome, otherwise it's just like installing the stock 2.8.<P>BTW....you can use the block from a front wheel drive 3.4, but you must rebuild it as the pistons and heads are not usable (pistons are dished and heads are aluminum.).  The advantage here is the starter holes are on the proper side....the disadvantage is more steps are needed to prepare the engine for installation.   ||||reg||6
Z||000003||Raydar||01-12-2000||09:55 AM||||Try this link <A HREF="http://welcome.to/thefierofactory" TARGET=_blank>http://welcome.to/thefierofactory</A> <BR>There is a section detailing exactly what needs to be done to install a 3.4.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 01-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||01-11-2000||09:18 PM||||I would still have to respectfully doubt that the 2.8 block could realisticly make 200 hp without forced induction. We have done a lot of things to a 2.8 but could not get the magic $2.00. We were even shaving the deck to bump up the compression. But you can believe what you want.   I would also like to know what part of the 3.4 block needs to be touched to clear an automatic tranny. There should be NO problems with clearance. All of the usual engine massaging tricks should be used if you really want a fantastic street car.||||reg||14
